get some coaching in how to teach school, but the emphasis was above all religious. More recently, a number of courses were set up by government and mission teachers were encouraged to enter for such courses. The A course is open to men with a Standard 6 education and takes one year. The B course is open to men with a Form 2 education and takes two years. The administration insists on an A certificate as a minimum qualification; in the Northern District, new appointees were usually expected to have a B certificate. The mission is less fortunate; in 1966 in the Northern District, only 97 out of 274 mission teachers had either an A or a B certificate. Out of the Sasembata staff of 12, five were trained; out of the Hohota staff of four, one was trained.\'  By the time of the eruption of Mt. Lamington, some Orokaiva communities were fairly familiar with Biblical themes: \'Certainly, the eruption would be followed by a resolve not to make the harsh power that brought the eruption angry again. Whether this power was primarily identified with God or with Sumbiripa is extremely hard to tell; it is even harder to tell whether these powers were viewed as being entirely distinct. Father Albert\'s testimony strongly suggests that the eruption was treated as symbolic of divine wroth as it was presented in the Bible.\'  But Schwimmer\'s other comments (see above) suggest significant time-lags in the spread of literacy.'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1127, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1008})
